The &lt;dfn&gt;collapse mechanism&lt;/dfn&gt; is triggered each time a bottle is removed. This action creates a void in the stack, which is then filled according to the subsequent recursive rules:
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;If no bottle is directly above the void, no change occurs. For instance, removing $F$.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;If a single bottle is directly above the void, it descends to fill the void, creating a new void. For instance, removing $D$.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;If two bottles are directly above the void, one descends to fill the void, creating a new void. For instance, removing $C$.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
This mechanism is recursive; for instance, if bottle $A$ is removed, either $B$ or $C$ can fill its place. If $C$ fills the void, then $D$ or $E$ can fill the void left by $C$. Thus, there are 3 distinct collapse mechanisms that can occur if $A$ is removed, even though the final configuration remains the same.
&lt;/p&gt;
