&lt;p&gt;Consider the following game, a quintessential representation of Combinatorial Game Theory:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Two competitors commence with a strip of $n$ unpainted squares and alternate turns.&lt;br /&gt;
During each turn, a competitor selects two adjacent unpainted squares and colors them black.&lt;br /&gt;
The competitor who is unable to execute a move is declared the loser.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;&lt;li&gt;$n = 1$: No permissible moves, hence the first competitor loses by default.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;$n = 2$: A single permissible move, post which the second competitor loses.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;$n = 3$: Two permissible moves, both resulting in a scenario where the second competitor loses.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;$n = 4$: Three permissible moves for the first competitor, who can secure a victory by coloring the two central squares.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;$n = 5$: Four permissible moves for the first competitor (illustrated below in red), but irrespective of the competitor's action, the second competitor (blue) emerges victorious.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;&lt;div class=&#34;center&#34;&gt;&lt;img src=&#34;project/images/p306_pstrip.gif&#34; class=&#34;dark_img&#34; alt=&#34;p306_pstrip.gif&#34; /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Thus, for $1 \le n \le 5$, there exist 3 values of $n$ where the first competitor can strategically ensure a win.&lt;br /&gt;
In a similar vein, for $1 \le n \le 50$, there exist 40 values of $n$ where the first competitor can strategically ensure a win.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For $1 \le n \le 1 000 000$, can you determine the number of $n$ values where the first competitor can strategically ensure a win?&lt;/p&gt;

