Consider a triangle that is dissected into four sections by two intersecting lines, each originating from a vertex and terminating at the opposing side. This process yields three smaller triangles and a quadrilateral. If the original triangle's area is an integer, it's often feasible to select the cuts such that all four sections also have integer areas. For instance, the illustration below depicts a triangle with an area of $55$ that has been dissected in this manner.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;div align=&#34;center&#34;&gt;&lt;img src=&#34;resources/images/0557-triangle.gif?1678992057&#34; alt=&#34;0557-triangle.gif&#34;&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
The areas are denoted as $a, b, c$ and $d$, in the above example, the individual areas are $a = 22$, $b = 8$, $c = 11$ and $d = 14$. It's also feasible to dissect a triangle of area $55$ such that $a = 20$, $b = 2$, $c = 24$, $d = 9$.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
Let's define a triangle cutting quadruple $(a, b, c, d)$ as a valid integer division of a triangle, where $a$ is the area of the triangle between the two cut vertices, $d$ is the area of the quadrilateral and $b$ and $c$ are the areas of the two other triangles, with the constraint that $b \le c$. The two solutions mentioned above are $(22,8,11,14)$ and $(20,2,24,9)$. These are the only two possible quadruples that have a total area of $55$.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
Let's denote $S(n)$ as the sum of the area of the uncut triangles represented by all valid quadruples with $a+b+c+d \le n$.&lt;br&gt; For instance, $S(20) = 259$.  
