&lt;p&gt;
In a game of cat and mouse, Tom (the cat) and Jerry (the mouse) are engaged in a chase on a simple graph $G$.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
Each vertex of $G$ represents a mousehole, and every edge of $G$ signifies a tunnel that links two mouseholes.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
At the start, Jerry is concealed in one of the mouseholes.&lt;br&gt;
Every dawn, Tom has the opportunity to inspect one (and only one) of the mouseholes. If Jerry is found hiding there, Tom captures Jerry, thus ending the game.&lt;br&gt;
Every dusk, if the game is still ongoing, Jerry relocates to a mousehole that is adjacent (i.e., connected by a tunnel, if one is available) to his current hideout. The subsequent morning, Tom resumes his search and the game proceeds in this manner.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
We define a graph $G$ as a &lt;dfn&gt;Tom graph&lt;/dfn&gt;, if our highly intelligent Tom, who is aware of the graph's configuration but ignorant of Jerry's location, can &lt;i&gt;assure&lt;/i&gt; to capture Jerry within a finite number of days.
Consider, for instance, all graphs on 3 nodes:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;div class=&#34;center&#34;&gt;
&lt;img src=&#34;resources/images/0690_graphs.jpg?1678992054&#34; alt=&#34;Graphs on 3 nodes&#34;&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
For graphs 1 and 2, Tom can capture Jerry in a maximum of three days. For graph 3, Tom can inspect the central connection on two successive days, thereby assuring to capture Jerry in a maximum of two days. These three graphs are hence Tom Graphs. However, graph 4 does not qualify as a Tom Graph because the game could potentially persist indefinitely.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
Let $T(n)$ denote the count of distinct Tom graphs with $n$ vertices. Two graphs are deemed identical if there exists a bijection $f$ between their vertices, such that $(v,w)$ is an edge if and only if $(f(v),f(w))$ is an edge.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
We have $T(3) = 3$, $T(7) = 37$, $T(10) = 328$ and $T(20) = 1416269$.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
Determine $T(2019)$, providing your answer modulo $1\,000\,000\,007$.
&lt;/p&gt;

