&lt;p&gt;Consider a character string $s$. We define $L(k,s)$ as the length of the longest substring of $s$ that appears at least $k$ times in $s$, or $0$ if no such substring exists. For instance, $L(3,\text{“bbabcabcabcacba”})=4$ due to the three appearances of the substring $\text{“abca”}$, and $L(2,\text{“bbabcabcabcacba”})=7$ due to the repeated substring $\text{“abcabca”}$. It's important to note that these occurrences can overlap.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Now, let's define $a_n$, $b_n$ and $c_n$ as the $0/1$ sequences as follows:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;&lt;li&gt;$a_0 = 0$&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;$a_{2n} = a_{n}$&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;$a_{2n+1} = 1-a_{n}$&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;$b_n = \lfloor\frac{n+1}{\varphi}\rfloor - \lfloor\frac{n}{\varphi}\rfloor$ (where $\varphi$ is the golden ratio)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;$c_n = a_n + b_n - 2a_nb_n$&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;&lt;p&gt;and $S_n$ is the character string $c_0\ldots c_{n-1}$. It is known that $L(2,S_{10})=5$, $L(3,S_{10})=2$, $L(2,S_{100})=14$, $L(4,S_{100})=6$, $L(2,S_{1000})=86$, $L(3,S_{1000}) = 45$, $L(5,S_{1000}) = 31$, and the sum of non-zero $L(k,S_{1000})$ for $k\ge 1$ equals $2460$.&lt;/p&gt;

