&lt;p&gt;A representative example of a titanic set is $S = \{(0, 0), (0, 1), (0, 2), (1, 1), (2, 0), (1, 0)\}$, in which the line that intersects the points $(0, 1)$ and $(2, 0)$ does not intersect any other point within $S$.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Contrarily, the set $\{(0, 0), (1, 1), (2, 2), (4, 4)\}$ does not qualify as a titanic set because the line that intersects any pair of points within the set also intersects the remaining points.&lt;/p&gt;

