For two &lt;i&gt;distinct&lt;/i&gt; primes $p$ and $q$, let's define $M(p,q,N)$ as the greatest positive integer $\le N$ that is exclusively divisible by both $p$ and $q$. If such a positive integer does not exist, we set $M(p,q,N)=0$.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
For instance, $M(2,3,100)=96$.&lt;br&gt; 
