&lt;p&gt;The &lt;strong&gt;lambda-calculus&lt;/strong&gt;, a universal computational model that forms the foundation of functional programming languages, is constructed on the basis of &lt;strong&gt;lambda-terms&lt;/strong&gt;. These lambda-terms, which constitute a minimal programming language, are characterized by the presence of only function definitions, function calls, and variables. The construction of lambda-terms adheres to the following principles:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A lambda-term can be any &lt;strong&gt;variable&lt;/strong&gt; $x$ (a single letter from an infinite alphabet).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;If $M$ and $N$ are lambda-terms, then $(M N)$, referred to as the &lt;strong&gt;application&lt;/strong&gt; of $M$ to $N$, is also a lambda-term.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;If $x$ is a variable and $M$ is a term, then $(\lambda x. M)$, known as an &lt;strong&gt;abstraction&lt;/strong&gt;, is a lambda-term. An abstraction is essentially an anonymous function that takes $x$ as a parameter and returns $M$.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A lambda-term $T$ is classified as &lt;strong&gt;closed&lt;/strong&gt; if all occurrences of any variable $x$ within $T$ are encompassed within an abstraction $(\lambda x. M)$ in $T$. The smallest abstraction that does this is said to &lt;strong&gt;bind&lt;/strong&gt; the occurrence of the variable $x$. In simpler terms, a lambda-term is closed if all its variables are bound to parameters of the functions that enclose them. For instance, the term $(\lambda x. x)$ is closed, while the term $(\lambda x. (x y))$ is not because $y$ is not bound.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Furthermore, variables can be renamed without altering the binding abstraction. This implies that $(\lambda x. x)$ and $(\lambda y. y)$ should be regarded as equivalent since only a parameter has been renamed. Terms that are equivalent modulo such renaming are referred to as &lt;i&gt;$\alpha$-equivalent&lt;/i&gt;. It should be noted that $(\lambda x. (\lambda y. (x y)))$ and $(\lambda x. (\lambda x. (x x)))$ are not $\alpha$-equivalent, as the abstraction binding the first variable was the outer one and becomes the inner one. However, $(\lambda x. (\lambda y. (x y)))$ and $(\lambda y. (\lambda x. (y x)))$ are $\alpha$-equivalent.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The table below consolidates the lambda-terms that can be written with a maximum of $15$ symbols, where symbols include parenthesis, $\lambda$, dot, and variables.&lt;/p&gt;

\[\begin{array}{|c|c|c|c|}
\hline
(\lambda x.x) &amp;amp; (\lambda x.(x x)) &amp;amp; (\lambda x.(\lambda y.x)) &amp;amp; (\lambda x.(\lambda y.y)) \\
\hline
(\lambda x.(x (x x))) &amp;amp; (\lambda x.((x x) x)) &amp;amp; (\lambda x.(\lambda y.(x x))) &amp;amp; (\lambda x.(\lambda y.(x y))) \\
\hline
(\lambda x.(\lambda y.(y x))) &amp;amp; (\lambda x.(\lambda y.(y y))) &amp;amp; (\lambda x.(x (\lambda y.x))) &amp;amp; (\lambda x.(x (\lambda y.y))) \\
\hline
(\lambda x.((\lambda y.x) x)) &amp;amp; (\lambda x.((\lambda y.y) x)) &amp;amp; ((\lambda x.x) (\lambda x.x)) &amp;amp; (\lambda x.(x (x (x x)))) \\
\hline
(\lambda x.(x ((x x) x))) &amp;amp; (\lambda x.((x x) (x x))) &amp;amp; (\lambda x.((x (x x)) x)) &amp;amp; (\lambda x.(((x x) x) x)) \\
\hline
\end{array}\]

&lt;p&gt;Let $\Lambda(n)$ denote the number of distinct closed lambda-terms that can be written using no more than $n$ symbols, where terms that are $\alpha$-equivalent to one another should be counted only once. It is given that $\Lambda(6) = 1$, $\Lambda(9) = 2$, $\Lambda(15) = 20$ and $\Lambda(35) = 3166438$.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Determine the value of $\Lambda(2000)$. Provide the answer modulo $1\,000\,000\,007$.&lt;/p&gt;

